      "title": "Electronegativity & Bond Type",
      "concept": {
        "summary": "M = metal   ·   NM = nonmetal   ·   I = ionic   ·   PC = polar covalent   ·   NPC = nonpolar covalent",
        "keyIdeas": [
          "ΔEN = higher EN − lower EN, so ΔEN is never negative.",
          "ΔEN under 0.40 is NPC. From 0.40 to 1.80 is PC. Over 1.80 is I."
        ]
      },
      "directions": "Element 1 is the first element written in the formula. Mark it M or NM. Look up both EN values in the chart at the bottom, subtract, and write ΔEN as a subtraction, like 2.96 − 2.20 = 0.76. Then use ΔEN to name the bond. Cl₂, O₂ and N₂ are elements, not compounds. Check yourself: a metal with a nonmetal usually comes out I, and two nonmetals come out PC or NPC. Rows 21–23 are close calls: let ΔEN decide.",
