      "keyNotes": [
        "EN values are Pauling values to two decimals (CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics). H, C, N, O, F, Na and Cl match the values already used in the course. Cutoffs follow OpenStax Chemistry 2e, the same as the U4–U5 slides.",
        "Rows 1–20 are clear: every ΔEN is at least 0.20 from a cutoff. Rows 21–23 are close calls added for discussion (Matt, 2026-11-10).",
        "CH₄: 0.35 is just under 0.40, so NPC. A one-decimal chart (C 2.5, H 2.1) gives exactly 0.4, so have students use the sheet's two-decimal values.",
        "HF: 1.78 is just under 1.80, so PC, even though F is the most electronegative element.",
        "AlCl₃: Al is a metal, yet ΔEN is 1.55, so PC. It breaks the metal + nonmetal rule of thumb: ΔEN decides. That is why the check line says “usually”.",
        "CO₂, CCl₄ and CF₄ have polar bonds but are nonpolar molecules because of their shape (5.4). This sheet asks about the bond only.",
        "PH₃: H (2.20) is higher than P (2.19), so the subtraction is 2.20 − 2.19. NCl₃ catches students out: N and Cl are close (0.12), so the bond is NPC."
      ]
